#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332899 :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ for the curve $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)=\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) & =0 \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y) & =0 \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(2,\\pi /4)} \\\\  & ={\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)}{x}\\right)}_{(2,\\pi /4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332901 :question/problem "![](images/fa/faa8a8e0521972fa860d474ac079405b6930df6c47fda05808fdf242d6d6922b.png)\n\nThe equation $xy=5$ represents a hyperbola. The tangent line at the point $(1,5)$ on the hyperbola intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the tangent line at the point $(1,5).$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y & =0 \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{-y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,5)$  is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(1,\\,5)} \\\\  & ={\\left(-\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}_{(1,\\,5)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{1} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,5)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-5) & =-5(x-1) \\\\ y & =-5(x-1)+5 \\\\ y & =-5x+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this tangent line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x+10 \\\\ y & =-5\\cdot 0+10 \\\\ y & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,10).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332914 :question/problem "![](images/f1/f1ae263b71fa17fd648464c24b307c064c9e657c2c9f6b36faf8c9819f1fb285.png)\n\nFind, to the nearest integer, the area of the parallelogram $ABCD.$" :question/worked-solution "The diagonal $\\overset{―}{AC}$ divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles. Therefore, the area of the parallelogram is twice the area of $△ACD$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/a6/a60b049aef53aef28802b084b3e445fd6253978500ca981d3b799977523cdd71.png)\n\n Computing the area of $△ACD,$ we find\n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ACD} & =\\frac{1}{2}(8)(5)\\operatorname{sin}{55}^{∘} \\\\  & =20\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{55}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 16.383,\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. Consequently,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABCD} & =2{\\mathcal{A}}_{ACD} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 16.383 \\\\  & =32.766.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, to the nearest integer, the area of the parallelogram is $33.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335761 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=xyz-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz),$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(1,0,\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz)-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)) \\\\  & =yz\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)) \\\\  & =yz\\cdot 1-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)) \\\\  & =yz-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}yz-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)) & =yz-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xz) \\\\  & =yz-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)\\cdot (z) \\\\  & =yz-z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz) \\\\  & =z(y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,0,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(1,0,\\pi )} & =\\pi (0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot \\pi )) \\\\  & =\\pi (0-(-1)) \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335762 :question/problem "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x,y)={e}^{xy}?$" :question/worked-solution "The level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nNotice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $(0,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider only level curves for $c>0.$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ {e}^{xy} & =c \\\\ xy & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡c \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡c}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can split this into three cases, depending on the sign of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡c.$\n\n- Case 1: $\\operatorname{ln}⁡c>0.$ In this case, we have the family of hyperbolas $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡c}{x}.$\n\n- Case 2: $\\operatorname{ln}⁡c=0.$ In this case, we have the lines $x=0$ and $y=0.$\n\n- Case 3: $\\operatorname{ln}⁡c<0.$ In this case, we have the family of hyperbolas $y=-\\frac{|\\operatorname{ln}⁡c|}{x}.$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of hyperbolas centered at the origin. Therefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/d3/d330ddb12d1df9d31af3d082b04271d02df381739064a6fe2203dabce358a09f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335768 :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P$ with $y$-coordinate $0$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\sqrt{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+\\frac{\\pi }{4})$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $y=0$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =\\sqrt{2}(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335769 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,1,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=2(x+y)-2$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =2(1+1)-2=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,1) & ={f}_{x}(1,1)(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =2(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =2x-2 \\\\ z & =2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,1,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=2x-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2x-1$ (or $2x-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=⟨2,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨2,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-i-2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $-\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(-\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})=\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335800 :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =xy,\\hspace{1em} &  & \\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=y\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}, &  &  \\\\ g(x,y) & =\\operatorname{ln}(xy),\\hspace{1em} &  & \\mathrm{∇}g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=g\\,∇\\,f+f\\,∇\\,g\n$$\n\nSo, using this product rule we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)+f(x,y)\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}(xy))(y\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})+xy(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{ln}(xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x\\operatorname{ln}(xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+y\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =[y\\operatorname{ln}(xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+y\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}]+[x\\operatorname{ln}(xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+x\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}] \\\\  & =y(\\operatorname{ln}(xy)+1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x(\\operatorname{ln}(xy)+1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335802 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}=\\frac{2\\cdot 0+0}{3\\cdot 0+0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$” is false. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=x}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x+x}{3x+x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x}{4x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{2}$ along the path $y=-x$” is true. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=-x}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-2x+x}{3x-x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-x}{2x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit exists and is equal to $0$” is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335804 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(-1,1,-1).$  \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$  and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z)\\,k \\\\  & =({y}^{2}+2xz)\\text{ }i+(2xy+{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+(2yz+{x}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-1,1,-1) & =((1)^{2}+2(-1)(-1))\\,i+(2(-1)(1)+(-1)^{2}))\\,j+(2(1)(-1)+(-1)^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,i-1j-1\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335809 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\operatorname{cos}t(1-\\operatorname{cos}t)-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}t(1-\\operatorname{cos}t)-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t & =\\operatorname{cos}t-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}t-({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t=1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}t(1-\\operatorname{cos}t)-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t=\\operatorname{cos}t-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335810 :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}t(\\operatorname{sin}t-1)+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}t(\\operatorname{sin}t-1)+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t-\\operatorname{sin}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t \\\\  & =({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t)-\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t=1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}t(\\operatorname{sin}t-1)+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t=1-\\operatorname{sin}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335811 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\operatorname{sin}\\theta {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ from the expression:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\\theta {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta )\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{cos}^{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\\theta {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335815 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\operatorname{sin}x{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x\\operatorname{cos}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ from the expression:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}x{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x)\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x) & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}x{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335816 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{5}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x$ from the expression:\n\n$$\n{\\operatorname{sin}}^{5}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x={\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x\\cdot ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x\\cdot ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x) & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\operatorname{sin}}^{5}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x={\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335817 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x$  from the first two terms:\n\n$$\n{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x\\cdot ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x=1$ to simplify the given expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x\\cdot ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x\\cdot 1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335818 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}t{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}t}{\\operatorname{cos}t}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}t{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}t}{\\operatorname{cos}t}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}t\\cdot ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t)}{\\operatorname{cos}t}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}t\\cdot ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t)}{\\operatorname{cos}t} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}t\\cdot 1}{\\operatorname{cos}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}t}{\\operatorname{cos}t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}t{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}t}{\\operatorname{cos}t}=\\operatorname{tan}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335819 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta {\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta }.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta {\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta }=\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )} & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta {\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta }=\\operatorname{cot}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335820 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\cdot ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\cdot ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }=\\operatorname{tan}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335821 :question/problem "$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x}+\\operatorname{sin}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x}+\\operatorname{sin}x & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{1} \\\\  & \\phantom{=}\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x(\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x)}{1(\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x(\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x)}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x(\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x)}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x} & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x)+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x}+\\operatorname{sin}x=\\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335863 :question/problem "What is the minimum value of $f(x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+\\pi )-4?$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+\\pi )\\le 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-1$ and then subtracting $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+\\pi )\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & \\ge -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+\\pi )\\ge -1 \\\\ -3 & \\ge -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+\\pi )-4\\ge -5 \\\\ -5 & \\le f(x)\\le -3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value is $-5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335928 :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=t+1,\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}-6,-3\\le t\\le 3,\n$$\n\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | ${\\color{blue}c}$ |\n| $y$ | ${\\color{blue}a}$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | ${\\color{blue}b}$ | $-5$ | $-2$ | $3$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a-b-c.$" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $a,$ we substitute $t=-3$ into $y={t}^{2}-6.$ This gives\n\n$$\na=(-3)^{2}-6=9-6=3.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $b,$ we substitute $t=0$ into $y={t}^{2}-6.$ This gives\n\n$$\nb={0}^{2}-6=0-6=-6.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $c,$ we substitute $t=3$ into $x=t+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\nc=3+1=4.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na-b-c=3-(-6)-4=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335939 :question/problem "![](images/48/488389632139d269e24958275ee20a9e5f8582fd0966e4d935487d238a463180.png)\n\nRounded to the nearest tenth, what is the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD?$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $△ABD$ and $△BDC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/68/68f5708d49577befe9bac6a7aaf6a54fcdb30dcd344367d2c69b8924093dbb61.png)\n\n \n\nFirst we find the area for $△ABD:$ \n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 12\\cdot 14\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{42.1}^{∘} \\\\  & =84\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{42.1}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 56.315\\hspace{0.167em}8,\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $△BCD:$ \n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{BDC} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 7\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{103.8}^{∘} \\\\  & =17.5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{103.8}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 16.994\\hspace{0.167em}8,\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABCD} & ={\\mathcal{A}}_{ABD}+{\\mathcal{A}}_{BDC} \\\\  & =56.315\\hspace{0.167em}8+16.994\\hspace{0.167em}8 \\\\  & =73.310\\hspace{0.167em}6\\end{aligned}$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest tenth, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $73.3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335940 :question/problem "![](images/b0/b0b100942a658966a5feea8a8677e7cfe86f21970a443385ff06034bbcb3d497.png)\n\nRounded to the nearest integer, what is the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD?$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $△ACD$ and $△ABC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/7e/7e3232258a523e4dbfebb6c380f5b88c2a54036b4d719e70ebf9060054f825d7.png)\n\n \n\nFirst we find the area for $△ACD:$ \n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ACD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 18\\cdot 21\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{60}^{∘} \\\\  & =189\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{60}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 163.679,\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $△ABC:$ \n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABC} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 36\\cdot 30\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{33}^{∘} \\\\  & =540\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{33}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 294.105,\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABCD} & ={\\mathcal{A}}_{ACD}+{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABC} \\\\  & =163.679+294.105 \\\\  & =457.784\\end{aligned}$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest integer, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $458.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335950 :question/problem "![](images/49/4969bf3cea8290c76e79419ed0160a8c1f92d7415250305df56eb8128ad15cf4.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx={t}^{4}+t-1,\\,y={t}^{3},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4{t}^{3}+1,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{4{t}^{3}+1}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}}{4(-1)^{3}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(-4)+1} \\\\  & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335953 :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{2t},\\,y=({t}^{2}-1){e}^{-t},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{2t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=(1+2t-{t}^{2}){e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{(1+2t-{t}^{2}){e}^{-t}}{2{e}^{2t}}=\\frac{1}{2}(1+2t-{t}^{2}){e}^{-3t}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+2(1)-(1)^{2}){e}^{-3(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+2-1){e}^{-3} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335959 :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=3t-2,\\,\\,\\,y=\\frac{10}{{t}^{2}+1},\\,-3\\le t\\le 3,\n$$\n\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | ${\\color{blue}a}$ | $-8$ | $-5$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $4$ | ${\\color{blue}c}$ |\n| $y$ | $1$ | ${\\color{blue}b}$ | $5$ | $10$ | $5$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $a,$ we substitute $t=-3$ into $x=3t-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\na=3(-3)-2=-9-2=-11.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $b,$ we substitute $t=-2$ into $y=\\frac{10}{{t}^{2}+1}.$ This gives\n\n$$\nb=\\frac{10}{(-2)^{2}+1}=\\frac{10}{5}=2.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $c,$ we substitute $t=3$ into $x=3t-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\na=3(3)-2=9-2=7.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-11+2+7=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335960 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+5,\\,\\,\\,y=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,t\\ne 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for ${t}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x={t}^{2}+5\\,⇒\\,{t}^{2}=x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting ${t}^{2}=x-5$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{x-5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{1}{x-5}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne 0$ and $x={t}^{2}+5,$ we must have $x\\ne 5.$ \n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{x-5},\\,x\\ne 5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335961 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-4},\\,\\,\\,y=2{t}^{2},\\,t\\ge 2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for ${t}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-4}\\,⇒\\,{t}^{2}={x}^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting ${t}^{2}={x}^{2}+4$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=2({x}^{2}+4)=2{x}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=2{x}^{2}+8.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ge 2.$ and $x=\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-4},$ we must have $x\\ge 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=2{x}^{2}+8,\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335963 :question/problem "![](images/5d/5d4abbff66a225f92a8e4f5d30fb86b0b8e205b8a047ed3ed9d5af7290ac89c6.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={t}^{5}-2t,\\,y=1-{t}^{3},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=5{t}^{4}-2,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-3{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{-3{t}^{2}}{5{t}^{4}-2}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{-3(-1)^{2}}{5(-1)^{4}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{5-2} \\\\  & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335964 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-3t,\\,y=2-t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=0$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=0$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={0}^{3}-3\\cdot 0=0-0=0, \\\\ y & =2-0=2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,2).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}-3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-1.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3{t}^{2}-3} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{3{t}^{2}-3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=0,$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=0} & =-\\,\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {0}^{2}-3} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{0-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\,}=-3\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,2)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2 & =-3(x-0) \\\\ y & =-3x+2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335971 :question/problem "![](images/de/de2b66f976dc204566516a1724fa19cc03bf8b8f4da1fa4f4a198fd656ad10ca.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $9$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(9,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$ centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=9.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335972 :question/problem "![](images/ec/ecda314aeaaa79332a51386926218175bb0ffd6ce0e564b069a643c53f23b642.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335973 :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $7$ centered at the origin?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=7.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335983 :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=4$ and ${b}^{2}=49.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=4,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{4}=2.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=49,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{49}=7.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=7,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=2.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 7=14,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335990 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}3\\sqrt{x} & {z}^{2} \\\\ 2y & -\\sqrt{x}\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3\\sqrt{x} & {z}^{2} \\\\ 2y & -\\sqrt{x}\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\sqrt{x}\\cdot (-\\sqrt{x})-{z}^{2}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =-3x-2y{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335991 :question/problem "Calculate $det(H)$, if\n$$\n\\displaystyle H=\\begin{bmatrix}-2r & 3s \\\\ -2t & 3u\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(H) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2r & 3s \\\\ -2t & 3u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2r\\cdot 3u-3s\\cdot (-2t) \\\\  & =-6ru-(-6st) \\\\  & =-6ru+6st \\\\  & =6(st-ru)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335992 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle S=\\begin{bmatrix}1-p & 1+p \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(S)=2$, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(S) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1-p & 1+p \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1-p)\\cdot 2-(1+p)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =2-2p+3+3p \\\\  & =5+p\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(S)=2$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(S) & =2 \\\\ 5+p & =2 \\\\ p & =2-5 \\\\ p & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336011 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $M$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-4-12)+2(-2+6)+1(4+4) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-16)+2\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =-16+8+8 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(M)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$M$ is a singular matrix” and $det(M)=0$ are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336013 :question/problem "![](images/9e/9eb144bd4109846f3e1af4fb7d8ed70d2d8a29c3804547b89238d6437c4258d8.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=3$ and the vertical radius has length $b=1.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
